prompt('Example 4.1',p('Let X count the weekly after-midnight awakenings in the textbook study. For 50 observations, the frequencies of x = 0,1,2,3,4,5 are 2,11,23,9,4,1. Construct a probability distribution and verify its two required properties.'))
answer('Example 4.1',table(['x','P(X=x)'],list(zip(range(6),['2/50','11/50','23/50','9/50','4/50','1/50'])))+p('Every probability is between 0 and 1, and their sum is 50/50 = 1. X has a finite, countable set of possible values.'))
answer('Try It 4.1','The possible values 0–5 form a discrete set. Each probability is in [0,1], and (4 + 8 + 16 + 14 + 6 + 2)/50 = 1. These verify a discrete probability distribution.')
answer('Try It 4.2','X = number of practices attended in a week. Its possible values are 0, 1, 2, with probabilities 0.02, 0.08, 0.90, respectively.')
prompt('Example 4.3','A soccer team plays 0, 1, or 2 days per week with probabilities 0.2, 0.5, 0.3. Construct an expected-value table and interpret the mean.')
answer('Example 4.3',table(['x','P(x)','xP(x)'],[(0,.2,0),(1,.5,.5),(2,.3,.6)])+p('μ = 0 + 0.5 + 0.6 = 1.1 days per week in the long run. It need not be a possible value in a single week.'))
answer('Try It 4.3','μ = 1(0.6) + 2(0.3) + 3(0.1) = 1.5 bottles per customer in the long run.')
prompt('Example 4.4','For x = 0,1,2,3,4,5, use probabilities 2/50,11/50,23/50,9/50,4/50,1/50. Find the expected number of weekly awakenings and the standard deviation.')
answer('Example 4.4','μ = [0(2)+1(11)+2(23)+3(9)+4(4)+5(1)]/50 = 105/50 = 2.1. Variance = Σ(x − 2.1)²P(x) = 0.1764 + 0.2662 + 0.0046 + 0.1458 + 0.2888 + 0.1682 = 1.05. Thus σ = √1.05 ≈ 1.0247 awakenings per week.')
answer('Try It 4.4','μ = [0(4) + 1(8) + 2(16) + 3(14) + 4(6) + 5(2)]/50 = 116/50 = 2.32 calls per 12-hour shift.')
prompt('Example 4.5','A $2 game selects five digits independently from 0–9. If your five guesses match in order, your $2 is returned and you profit $100,000. Otherwise you lose $2. Find expected net profit per play.')
answer('Example 4.5','P(win) = (1/10)⁵ = 0.00001. P(lose) = 0.99999. Expected net profit = 100000(0.00001) − 2(0.99999) = −$0.99998, approximately a $1 loss per play over many plays.')
answer('Try It 4.5','P(all four suits correct) = (1/4)⁴ = 1/256. Net profit is $256 on a win and −$1 otherwise. E(X) = 256(1/256) − 1(255/256) = 1/256 dollar ≈ $0.003906 per play. The stated prize includes returning the entry fee.')
prompt('Try It 4.6','A spinner has probabilities 2/5 red, 2/5 blue, and 1/5 green. Net outcomes are −$10, $0, and $10, respectively. Complete a table of x, P(x), and xP(x), then calculate expected net profit.')
answer('Try It 4.6',table(['Color','x','P(x)','xP(x)'],[('Red',-10,'2/5',-4),('Blue',0,'2/5',0),('Green',10,'1/5',2)])+p('Expected net profit = −4 + 0 + 2 = −$2 per play.'))
answer('Try It 4.7',table(['X: odd cards','P(X)','XP(X)'],[(0,'1/4',0),(1,'1/2','1/2'),(2,'1/4','1/2')])+p('Each independent stack has four odd and four even cards. μ = 1. Variance = (0−1)²(1/4) + (1−1)²(1/2) + (2−1)²(1/4) = 1/2. σ = √(1/2) ≈ 0.7071.'))
answer('Try It 4.8',f'Use the supplied historical probability p = 0.0108. X is 100 with probability p and −10 with probability 1−p. μ = 100p − 10(1−p) = {110*.0108-10:.3f} dollars. σ = 110√[p(1−p)] ≈ {110*sqrt(.0108*.9892):.4f} dollars.')
prompt('Example 4.9','In the textbook’s model, 30% of students withdraw from a physics course. Define “success,” the success probability, and a random variable counting successes in a sample of n students.')
answer('Example 4.9','Define success as withdrawal, with p = 0.30 and q = 0.70. X counts withdrawals among the n sampled students. Calling an outcome a success does not mean it is desirable. A binomial model also needs independent trials and constant p.')
answer('Try It 4.9','One natural choice is “the school offers fruit daily,” so p = 0.48 and q = 0.52. You may instead define the opposite as success if you consistently use p = 0.52.')
prompt('Example 4.10','Each of 20 independent games is won with probability 0.55. Define X, state its support and model, and express the probability of exactly 15 wins.')
answer('Example 4.10',f'X = number of wins; X ∼ B(20,0.55), with values 0–20. P(X=15) = C(20,15)(0.55)¹⁵(0.45)⁵ ≈ {binom.pmf(15,20,.55):.4f}.')
answer('Try It 4.10','Let X count successful catches in 20 attempts. Assuming independent attempts with the same success probability, X ∼ B(20,0.35); the question is P(X = 12).')
prompt('Example 4.11','A biased coin has P(heads) = 0.25. Flip it independently five times. Construct the probability distribution for X = number of heads, sketch its bar graph, and find P(X > 3).')
answer('Example 4.11',table(['x','P(X=x)'],[(k,f'{binom.pmf(k,5,.25):.6f}') for k in range(6)])+chart('Binomial probabilities: n=5, p=0.25',list(range(6)),[('Probability',[float(binom.pmf(k,5,.25)) for k in range(6)])],ylabel='Probability')+p('P(X>3) = P(4)+P(5) = 0.0146484375 + 0.0009765625 = 0.015625 ≈ 0.0156. The mean is np = 1.25. The coin is biased, not fair.'))
answer('Try It 4.11','X = number of ones in ten independent rolls. X ∼ B(10,1/6). The question is P(X > 3), equivalently 1 − P(X ≤ 3).')
answer('Try It 4.12','There is a fixed sample of 50, and each result is pass-on-first-try or not. Under the model, p = 0.65 is constant and trials are approximately independent. Those last two assumptions are required as well as the first two features.')
prompt('Example 4.13','Assume 41% of adult workers have a high-school diploma and no further education. In 20 independent selections, find P(at most 12 such workers), the expected count, and its standard deviation.')
answer('Example 4.13',f'X ∼ B(20,0.41). P(X≤12) = Σ from k=0 to 12 of C(20,k)(0.41)ᵏ(0.59)²⁰⁻ᵏ ≈ {binom.cdf(12,20,.41):.4f}. μ = np = 8.2; σ = √(npq) = √[20(0.41)(0.59)] ≈ {sqrt(20*.41*.59):.4f}.')
